<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
    Hardened-runtime entitlements for Roost-Iced.app (M6 6a evaluation
    bundle — crates/roost-iced, `mac/scripts/bundle-iced.sh`).

    Effective when the bundle is code-signed (ad-hoc or Developer ID).
    bundle-iced.sh signs with the `runtime` option, mirroring
    bundle.sh / Roost.entitlements.

    com.apple.security.device.audio-input
    com.apple.security.device.camera
    com.apple.security.automation.apple-events

    Roost-Iced is the TCC *responsible app* for the programs it hosts:
    when a child process in a tab (Claude Code's /voice, ffmpeg, sox,
    an osascript automation) touches the microphone, camera, or sends
    Apple events, macOS attributes the request to Roost-Iced, not the
    child. Under the hardened runtime that attribution is *silently
    denied* unless Roost-Iced carries the matching resource
    entitlement — no prompt, no Privacy pane entry, no error. These
    three let those requests prompt ("A program running in Roost-Iced
    would like to use the microphone") instead of failing silently.
    The paired purpose strings live in Info-iced.plist.template. cf.
    Roost.entitlements, ghostty, and cmux, which all carry the same
    capture set.

    com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation

    The case this file previously reserved the entitlement for has
    arrived (plan 028, M6 6c): bundle-iced.sh now embeds an
    ad-hoc-signed Sparkle.framework, dlopen'd at runtime under the
    hardened runtime. Library validation insists every loaded library
    be signed by the SAME Team ID as the main executable (or be a
    platform binary). Roost-Iced ships ad-hoc-signed (no Team ID
    yet), so without this entitlement the hardened runtime would
    refuse the framework — for a dlopen that's a load failure at
    updater-init time (the link-time case aborts the whole launch
    with a code-signature (LV) kill; cf. Roost.entitlements, same
    entitlement for the same embedded framework).

    REMOVE THIS once the bundle is team-signed (Developer ID +
    notarization, the Swift side's #83 equivalent): when Roost-Iced
    and Sparkle.framework are both signed by our Team ID, same-team
    signing satisfies library validation and the entitlement is no
    longer needed (cf. Ghostty, which is team-signed and carries no
    LV-disable). Keeping it past that point needlessly weakens the
    hardened runtime.

    Release Developer-ID builds (release.yml's `mac-iced` job, plan
    030) are already team-signed today and STILL carry this
    entitlement: team-signing alone hasn't triggered its removal —
    that's a separate entitlements hygiene pass, not yet done.

    What we still INTENTIONALLY do not need (same reasoning as
    Roost.entitlements):

    * `cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory` / `cs.allow-jit`
      libghostty-vt is linked as a static archive, no JIT, no
      dynamically generated machine code at runtime.

    * `network.client` / `network.server` Roost-Iced does not speak
      over a network socket; the IPC server is UNIX-domain only.

    * App Sandbox flags sandboxing the shells Roost-Iced spawns would
      defeat the product.

    * com.apple.security.personal-information.{addressbook,calendars,
      location,photos-library} — same blast-radius reasoning as
      Roost.entitlements: a TCC grant is inherited by every child
      process this app ever spawns, so we add a PIM key only if a
      concrete Roost feature needs it, not speculatively.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>com.apple.security.device.audio-input</key>
  <true/>
  <key>com.apple.security.device.camera</key>
  <true/>
  <key>com.apple.security.automation.apple-events</key>
  <true/>
  <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
  <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
